<command>
  <title>MinGBoverZZ [PROTOTYPE]</title>
  <short_description>[PROTOTYPE] minimal Groebner basis of polys over ZZ</short_description>
<syntax>
MinGBoverZZ(L: <type>LIST</type> of <type>RINGELEM</type>): <rtn>LIST</rtn>
</syntax>
<description>
This function returns a minimal Groebner basis of the ideal generated by <tt>L</tt>,
which must be a list of polynomials from a ring with coefficient ring <ttref>ZZ</ttref>.
<em>This is just a prototype:</em> exact semantics, and interface are likely to change!
<example>
/**/  use ZZ[x,y];
/**/ MinGBoverZZ([x^2+y^2+2, x^3-y^3, x*y-2*x-3]);
[1460,  4*y +308,  x +499*y +500,  y^2 -6*x +4*y -3]
</example>
</description>
<seealso>
  <see>GBasis</see>
</seealso>
<keys>
  <key>GB over the integers</key>
</keys>
</command>
<!-- ===  COMMAND  =============================================== -->
<command>
  <title>MinGens</title>
  <short_description>list of minimal generators</short_description>
<syntax>
MinGens(M: <type>IDEAL</type>|<type>MODULE</type>): <rtn>LIST</rtn>
</syntax>
<description>
If <tt>M</tt> is a homogeneous ideal or module, this function returns
a list of minimal generators for <tt>M</tt> (not necessarily a subset
of <tt>gens(M)</tt>).
<par/>
For non-homogeneous input use <ttref>MinSubsetOfGens</ttref>.
<par/>
NOTE: the coefficient ring must be a field.
<example>
/**/  use R ::= QQ[x,y,z];
/**/  I := ideal(x-y, (x-y)^4, z+y, (z+y)^2);
/**/  MinGens(I);
[y + z, x + z]

/**/  R3 := NewFreeModule(R, 3);
/**/  MGens := matrix(R, [[x,y,z], [x^2,0,z^2], [2*x^2,x*y,z^2+x*z]]);
/**/  M := SubmoduleRows(R3, MGens);
/**/  gens(M);
[[x, y, z],  [x^2, 0, z^2],  [2*x^2, x*y, x*z +z^2]]
/**/  MinGens(M);
[[x, y, z],  [0, x*y, x*z -z^2]]
</example>
</description>
<keys>
  <key>minimal generators of monomial ideal</key>
</keys>
<seealso>
  <see>IdealOfMinGens</see>
  <see>SubmoduleOfMinGens</see>
  <see>MinSubsetOfGens</see>
</seealso>
<types>
  <type>groebner</type>
  <type>groebner-basic</type>
</types>
</command>
